diff --git a/domains/findings/pages/a-write-and-an-audit-typecheck-different-programs.finding.ts b/domains/findings/pages/a-write-and-an-audit-typecheck-different-programs.finding.ts
index d074eb554fea4b2c8fd16c8afd9da8b9af70dbd1..ca5d4b0309a49b26d31f2429452367d73a2f411d 100644
--- a/domains/findings/pages/a-write-and-an-audit-typecheck-different-programs.finding.ts
+++ b/domains/findings/pages/a-write-and-an-audit-typecheck-different-programs.finding.ts
@@ -8,5 +8,5 @@ export const aWriteAndAnAuditTypecheckDifferentPrograms = {
   claim:
     "A write and an audit build different TypeScript programs, so a change reading clean at a write can refuse at an audit. A write roots the changed files and their import closure. An audit roots every file. Since commit `9a40fb9fa1` a write also roots every `.d.ts` the index names, so a declaration file no longer differs between the two. Every other file no import reaches still does, a `.ts` module carrying `declare global` above all.",
   evidence:
-    "In `akasha/checks/code-checks/pages/typecheck/typecheck.code-check.code.ts`, `reachedBy` walks import edges out of `seeds = [...change.changed, ...landingsIn(change)]`, and `rootsOf` filters that to what the change leaves behind. `programOver` in `code-system/code-typing/code-typing.module.code.ts:148` hands the roots to `ts.createProgram` as `rootNames`. An audit takes a different change: `everythingIn` in `checks/modules/change-walking/change-walking.module.code.ts:221` answers `changed: everyFileIn(root)`, so every file is a root and the program is the whole tree.\n\nWhat is mended. Commit `9a40fb9fa1` added `declaringIn(change, index)` at lines 76 to 79, filtering `index.everyPath()` to the compiled paths ending `.d.ts` the change leaves behind. Line 146 is now `const named = [...new Set([...roots, ...declaringIn(change, shadow.index)])]`, and `named` is what the program is rooted at. The loop gathering diagnostics at line 154 still runs over `roots` alone, so a declaration file is compiled with the change and never judged by it. An ambient declaration in a `.d.ts` is therefore present at a write and at an audit alike, and a collision between two of them is refused at the write landing the second.\n\nWhat is not mended. The divergence itself is untouched, and still runs one way: anything reached by no import and not named `.d.ts` is in the audit program and not in the write program. A `declare global` block inside an ordinary `.ts` module is exactly that.\n\nThe measurement opening this finding, kept for what the divergence costs. Thirteen addon packages each landed a `declare global` module meeting its own write, because at write only that package was in the program. At audit all thirteen were, `EVENT_MANAGER` and `$multi` declared six times over and `GetString` four, for over 245 refusals, mostly `TS2451 Cannot redeclare block-scoped variable`. No change held two of those files, so no write gate could see the collision.",
+    "In `akasha/checks/code-checks/pages/typecheck/typecheck.code-check.code.ts`, `reachedBy` walks import edges out of `seeds = [...change.changed, ...landingsIn(change)]`, and `rootsOf` filters that to what the change leaves behind. `programOver` in `code/code-typing/code-typing.module.code.ts:148` hands the roots to `ts.createProgram` as `rootNames`. An audit takes a different change: `everythingIn` in `checks/modules/change-walking/change-walking.module.code.ts:221` answers `changed: everyFileIn(root)`, so every file is a root and the program is the whole tree.\n\nWhat is mended. Commit `9a40fb9fa1` added `declaringIn(change, index)` at lines 76 to 79, filtering `index.everyPath()` to the compiled paths ending `.d.ts` the change leaves behind. Line 146 is now `const named = [...new Set([...roots, ...declaringIn(change, shadow.index)])]`, and `named` is what the program is rooted at. The loop gathering diagnostics at line 154 still runs over `roots` alone, so a declaration file is compiled with the change and never judged by it. An ambient declaration in a `.d.ts` is therefore present at a write and at an audit alike, and a collision between two of them is refused at the write landing the second.\n\nWhat is not mended. The divergence itself is untouched, and still runs one way: anything reached by no import and not named `.d.ts` is in the audit program and not in the write program. A `declare global` block inside an ordinary `.ts` module is exactly that.\n\nThe measurement opening this finding, kept for what the divergence costs. Thirteen addon packages each landed a `declare global` module meeting its own write, because at write only that package was in the program. At audit all thirteen were, `EVENT_MANAGER` and `$multi` declared six times over and `GetString` four, for over 245 refusals, mostly `TS2451 Cannot redeclare block-scoped variable`. No change held two of those files, so no write gate could see the collision.",
 } as const satisfies Finding
